        <div class="review">
            <div class="review-icon">📋</div>
            <div>
                <strong>Phase 14 Build Note — Foundation for Phase 15</strong>
                <p>
                    Phase 14 is the first physical prototype. The goal is not a finished vehicle — it is a validated mechanical
                    proof of concept that confirms three things: (1) the dual-acting pistons produce enough pressure after their
                    own stroke to meaningfully drive the rails, (2) the rhombic drives produce sufficient electricity to cover
                    the vehicle's ~150W electrical demand without a supplementary generator, and (3) the rail housing converts
                    reciprocating steam pressure into continuous shaft rotation with acceptable torque ripple.
                </p>
                <p style="margin-top:.8rem">
                    If T-3 shows rhombic output comfortably exceeds 150W demand, no front generator is needed and the design
                    stays minimal. If rhombic output is marginal, a small automotive alternator mounts at the front engine
                    housing output shaft — adding approximately $40 and 3kg. This decision is made after bench test results,
                    not before.
                </p>
                <p style="margin-top:.8rem">
                    Phase 15 should focus on: low-speed track testing 0–30 mph with full PCW sensor logging ·
                    water consumption measurement per 100 miles · brake certification · first 100-mile endurance run ·
                    refinement of rail phase offset based on observed torque ripple ·
                    and assessment of whether the hydrostatic transmission needs an accumulator to buffer high-idle pressure surges.
                    The Chevy Steamer is real at Phase 14. Phase 15 makes it drive.
                </p>
            </div>
